      "The research agent's finding, independently re-verified by reading every cited line before implementing anything: resendBundleBuyerEmailAction (src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts, the operator's manual row-level remedy for isBundleHandoffMissingBuyerEmail) computed needsReceipt/needsDns from a single stale getDeployHandoffBySlug read at the top of the function, sent the real receipt/DNS emails gated only on that stale check, and persisted the durable sent-marker notes afterward via updateDeployHandoffStatus with no atomicity between the check and the write. Its own doc comment claims to mirror resendCommerceClientReceiptAction, but only copied that sibling's sequential-retry behavior, not its atomic race guard: resendCommerceClientReceiptAction's marker write (markCommerceOrderClientReceiptSent) is a single SQL UPDATE ... WHERE clientReceiptEmailSentAt IS NULL OR = '' (src/lib/db/sqlite-queries.ts's sqliteMarkCommerceOrderClientReceiptSent, and the matching Convex mutation's query-then-patch check) that returns whether THIS call actually won the race, and the action branches on that to warn the operator a duplicate may have gone out. resendBundleBuyerEmailAction had no equivalent — a concurrent invocation (a second open dashboard tab, or a double-click landing before the button's isPending-disabled state takes effect, the same threat model this file's sibling actions — Buffer scheduling, commerce Mark paid out, commerce client receipt resend — all explicitly call out in their own comments) could read the same stale 'not yet sent' snapshot, send its own real duplicate transactional email to the end customer, and both concurrent calls would silently report success: true, hiding the duplicate send entirely rather than surfacing it. The per-IP rate limit on this action (bundle-buyer-email-resend, 20/60s) does nothing to prevent this since two requests easily fit in that window. Shipped: resendBundleBuyerEmailAction now decouples 'did we actually send' (sentReceipt/sentDns, set immediately after each Resend call) from 'what do we persist' — after both sends complete, it re-reads the handoff via a second getDeployHandoffBySlug call (mirroring the re-read-then-merge TOCTOU pattern already used by scheduleMarketingPackToBufferAction/archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProductsAction/preserveCommerceStripeIds) and re-checks hasBundleReceiptEmailNote/hasBundleDnsHandoffEmailNote against the fresh notes for each half this call itself just sent. A marker already present on the fresh read means a concurrent call recorded it first: that half's duplicate note is not re-appended (avoiding notes-array duplication), and the action returns success: false with an explicit 'may have sent a duplicate notification email' warning naming which half(es) raced, exactly mirroring resendCommerceClientReceiptAction's own concurrent-race message. When neither half raced, behavior is unchanged from before (both markers persist normally, success: true). When every marker this call sent already raced, updateDeployHandoffStatus is not called at all (nothing new to persist) and the freshly-read handoff is returned instead. This cannot un-send a duplicate email already in flight — same limitation the sibling commerce guard already documents — but it stops silently reporting a false, unconditional success on the exact class of race this codebase has repeatedly hardened against elsewhere. Added 3 new Vitest cases to src/app/dashboard/leads/resend-bundle-buyer-email-action.test.ts (receipt-marker race, DNS-marker race, both-markers race with zero updateDeployHandoffStatus calls) using mockResolvedValueOnce sequencing on getDeployHandoffBySlug to simulate the concurrent write landing between the initial read and the fresh re-check; all 15 pre-existing cases in that file pass unchanged (the fresh re-check reads the same stale-empty-notes mock by default, so no race is detected in the ordinary path). Verified the new tests actually catch the regression: git stash'd actions.ts alone (test file kept) and re-ran the suite — all 3 new cases failed with the exact pre-fix symptom (silently reported success: true) before restoring via git stash pop and confirming a clean, intended-only diff. Added a paragraph to /docs/architecture directly after the existing resendBundleBuyerEmailAction writeup naming the gap and the fix. Verified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run src/app/dashboard/leads/resend-bundle-buyer-email-action.test.ts (18 passed, up from 15) + npx vitest run full suite (3345 passed, up from 3342, 209 files unchanged) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same 43-route split) + npm run dev smoke check curling /dashboard/leads, /preview/demo-cafe-blue-river-salon, and /docs/architecture (200 on all three) since this touches a DB-persisting dashboard action feeding the bundle-order-missing-buyer-email due-queue. Killed the dev server, removed the stray gitignored local.db it created, and confirmed git status showed only the three intended source files changed before this process-log append. NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this closes a real, previously-unaudited TOCTOU duplicate-send race in an already-shipped dashboard action, not any of the five gated items. Diff-gate anchor moves to ec0f4411 (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#828).",
